On Wednesday, 4 June 2014 17:52:22 UTC+2, Elias T. Krainski wrote:
>
> We can start by
>   y_t = x_t + e_t
>   x_t = r x_{t-1} + w_t
> and them re-write the state equation as
>   0 = x_t - r x_{t-1} - w_t
> and in this last equation we have weights
>   1 for x_t
>  -1 for x_{t-1}
>  -1 for w_t
>

I was going to start a new thread, but I thought it would be easier to follow up on this one...

Anyway, if I've understood the state-space models in INLA correctly, the examples all fit 0 = x_t - r x_{t-1} - w_t with known r. But I'm interested in problems where I want to estimate r, and even have r as a function of more covariates. Is this possible with a state-space formulation?

FWIW, my models are more complicated, but I think I can work out what to do from writing an AR(1) this way, and work it out from there.

Bob

